      "title": "Individual disability income",
      "narration": "Rosa pays her own disability premium. She may not deduct it; disability income insurance is not a medical expense for tax purposes, because it replaces income rather than paying for care. In exchange, every dollar of benefit she receives while disabled is free of federal income tax. That is why insurers cap benefits at 60 to 70 percent of gross income: tax-free benefits at that level approach her take-home pay. If Rosa later deducts the premium as a business expense, which the law does not permit for her own coverage, the benefits would become taxable. Keep the premium after-tax and the benefits stay clean.",
